      ":question/id": "77c74a84-122e-5dd7-b170-e7f0507d8abe",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\n$\\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{2}}{z^{4}}\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{2}}{z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{2}}{z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$.",
