      ":question/problem": "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\n$\\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{3}}{z^{5}}\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{3}}{z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\log\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)-5\\log\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{3}}{z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\log\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)-5\\log\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$.",
