      ":question/id": "41bba485-231e-5eaa-99c4-61446cde6137",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Assume $x>0$.\n\nExpress $3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)$ as a single logarithm.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the power rule for logarithms, rewrite the powered numerical term and evaluate the power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& 3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(2^{3}\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(8\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{3}\\left(8\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(8x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms and simplifying the numerical factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{3}\\left(8x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{8x}{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(2x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& 3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(2x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x>0$.",
